        {
          "id": 2761352602,
          "type": "DiffNote",
          "body": "As a first pass, these changes look good.  In general, if the field label is the same (or nearly so, ignoring trivial case differences such as \"Record length\" vs \"Record Length\"), then `proto_tree_add_foo_format_value()` should be used instead of `proto_tree_add_foo_format()`, with the removal of the field label plus the ':' character and any leading whitespace from the format string, of course.\n\nNow this particular change does illustrate the need for a second pass though, as any format specifier that consists entirely of \"%s\" means there's no need for a `proto_tree_add_string_format_value()` function at all, but as @alagoutte mentions, `proto_tree_add_string()` (or even `proto_tree_add_item()`) should be used instead.  The same holds for other types as well.  For example, if a format specifier for `proto_tree_add_uint_format_value()` is just \"%u\", or \"%d\" for `proto_tree_add_int_format_value()`, etc., then they too should be replaced either by `proto_tree_add_item()` or by the appropriate `proto_tree_add_foo()` function.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6624779,
            "username": "cjmaynard",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Christopher Maynard",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://gitlab.com/uploads/-/system/user/avatar/6624779/avatar.png?v=1788529529",
